Let's denote the angle formed between the lines with slopes $2$ and $\frac{1}{3}$ as $\theta$. $\text{The formula for the tangent of the angle between two lines with slopes $m_1$ and $m_2$ is given by:} $

\tan{\theta} = \left|\frac{m_1 - m_2}{1 + m_1m_2}\right| 

\text{Substituting the given slopes:}
\tan{\theta} = \left|\frac{2 - \frac{1}{3}}{1 + (2)(\frac{1}{3})}\right| = 1

\text{Since the angle is acute, we can assume that $\theta$ is smaller than half of the full angle in terms of $\tan^{-1}$. Thus, the angle $\theta$ formed by the lines is:} 

\boxed{\angle \theta=45^\circ}

\begin{align*}
m_1 = 2, \ m_2 = \frac{1}{3} \\
\text{Angle between lines} = \theta \\
\tan \theta = \left|\frac{m_1 - m_2}{1 + m_1 m_2}\right| \\
\end{align*}
Since both lines are between \(0\) and \(180^\circ\), the absolute value has no effect, so:
\begin{align*}
\theta &= \tan^{-1}\left(\left|\frac{2 - \frac{1}{3}}{1 + (2)(\frac{1}{3})}\right|\right) \\
&= \tan^{-1}\left(\left|\frac{\frac{5}{3}}{\frac{5}{3}}\right|\right) \\
&= \tan^{-1}\left(1\right) \\
&= \frac{\pi}{4} \\
&= 45^\circ
\end{align*}
