Let \( P(k) \) be the probability that Flora will eventually land at position 10 after exactly \( k \) jumps. Clearly, \( P(k) = 0 \) for \( k < 10 \).

For \( k \geq 10 \), we can express \( P(k) \) as the sum of the probabilities of the frog landing at position 10 after making a jump of \( m \) on the previous step:
\[ P(k) = \sum_{m=1}^{10} \frac{1}{2^m} P(k-m) \]

To find \( P(10) \), we use the recursion:
\[ P(10) = \sum_{m=1}^{10} \frac{1}{2^m} P(10-m) \]

We now expand this step by step up to \( P(25) \) (since we are looking for \( P(10) \), and the recursion should end at some \( k \geq 10 \)):

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \frac{1}{2} P(9) + \frac{1}{4} P(8) + \frac{1}{8} P(7) + \frac{1}{16} P(6) + \frac{1}{32} P(5) \\
       & + \frac{1}{64} P(4) + \frac{1}{128} P(3) + \frac{1}{256} P(2) + \frac{1}{512} P(1) + \frac{1}{1024} P(0) \tag{1}
\end{align*}

Continuing this expansion and considering we will stop evaluating \( P(a) \) when \( a < 0 \), we get:

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \frac{1}{32768} \left( \sum_{a=0}^{9} \frac{1}{2^a} P(a) \right) \tag{2}
\end{align*}

Notice that \( P(0) = 0 \) because the frog cannot jump from position 0 to land at position 10 in one jump (position 10 is at least 2 more positions away than 0).

Expanding terms and considering only the non-zero terms:

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \frac{1}{32768} \left( \frac{1}{2} P(9) + \frac{1}{4} P(8) + \frac{1}{8} P(7) + \frac{1}{16} P(6) + \frac{1}{32} P(5) \right) \tag{3}
\end{align*}

From \( (3) \), we can see the pattern:

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \frac{1}{2^k} P(10-k) \quad \text{for } k = 1, 2, 3, \ldots, 9 \tag{4}
\end{align*}

Applying \( P(10) \) recursively, we notice that there are terms like \( P(10-9), P(10-10) \):

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \sum_{k=0}^{9} \frac{1}{2^{10-k}} \cdot \sum_{m=1}^{k} \frac{1}{2^m} P(10-m) \tag{5}
\end{align*}

This pattern continues, leading to a simpler expression.

Notice that, by the time \( P(10-k) \) for \( k < 0 \) is zero, the remaining terms \( P(0) \), \( P(1) \), \( P(2) \), etc. become all null after accounting for \( k \leq 0 \). Hence, we stop at:

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \sum_{k=0}^{9} \frac{1}{2^{10-k}} \left( \frac{1}{2} \sum_{m=1}^{1} \frac{1}{2^m} + \frac{1}{4} \sum_{m=1}^{2} \frac{1}{2^m} + \cdots + \frac{1}{2^{10-k}} \cdot 1 \right) \tag{6}
\end{align*}

We sum the series \( \sum_{j=1}^{i} \frac{1}{2^j} \) for \( i \leq 10-k \):

\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \sum_{k=0}^{9} \frac{1}{2^{10-k}} \left( \sum_{j=1}^{i} \frac{1}{2^j} \right) \tag{7}
\end{align*}

The series simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=1}^{i} \frac{1}{2^j} & = 1 - \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^i \tag{8}
\end{align*}

Substituting this into \( (7) \) gives:
\begin{align*}
P(10) & = \sum_{k=0}^{9} \frac{1}{2^{10-k}} \left[ 1 - \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^i \right] \tag{9}
\end{align*}

