Following the same pattern of substitution, the inequality now reads:
\begin{align*}
(x - 1) + (1 - y) \le 1
\end{align*}
Simplifying gives:
\begin{align*}
x - y \le 1
\end{align*}

### Case 3: \(x \le 1\) and \(y \ge 1\)
Replacing \(x\) with \(-x\) (noting that \(|x - 1| = |-x - 1|\)), we have:
\begin{align*}
(-x - 1) + (y - 1) \le 1
\end{align*}
Therefore, this inequality simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
x - y \le 3
\end{align*}

### Case 4: \(x \le 1\) and \(y \le 1\)
Lastly, for when \(x\) and \(y\) are both less than or equal to 1, the inequality becomes:
\begin{align*}
(-x - 1) + (-y - 1) \le 1
\end{align*}
Which simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
x + y \ge 3
\end{align*}
However, since \(x \le 1\) and \(y \le 1\), the condition \(x + y \ge 3\) is not possible.

